{"qid":"Q227","names":["Republic of Azerbaijan"],"title":"Republic of Azerbaijan","endonym":{"native":"Azərbaycan","lang":"az"},"capsule":"The Azerbaijan Democratic Republic, proclaimed on 28 May 1918 in the aftermath of the Russian Revolution and the collapse of the Transcaucasian federation, was the first secular parliamentary republic in the Turkic and Muslim worlds. Led by Mammad Amin Rasulzade's Musavat party, it founded Baku State University, extended equal voting rights to women, and held 145 parliamentary sessions before receiving Allied de facto recognition in January 1920. The Soviet XI Red Army crossed the border in April 1920, and parliament surrendered on 28 April to avoid mass bloodshed, establishing the Azerbaijan Soviet Socialist Republic.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial claims were disputed from the outset with Armenia (over Karabakh and Zangezur) and Georgia; the ADR's effective control varied significantly by region and is only partially mappable.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Azerbaijan Democratic Republic","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azerbaijan_Democratic_Republic"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Mammad Amin Rasulzade","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mammad_Amin_Rasulzade"},{"label":"Britannica: Azerbaijan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Azerbaijan"}],"britannica":"place/Azerbaijan","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Azerbaijan","rulers":[{"qid":"Q2468588","name":"Hasan Hasanov","start":1991,"end":1992,"image":"Hasan Hasanov, 2010 (cropped).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Hasan%20Hasanov%2C%202010%20(cropped).j